        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="318" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;html=1;exitX=0;exitY=0.5;entryX=1;entryY=0.5;strokeWidth=2;dashed=1;strokeColor=#666666;" edge="1" parent="1" source="243" target="241">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"><Array as="points"><mxPoint x="1540" y="2220"/><mxPoint x="1540" y="2026"/></Array></mxGeometry>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="318l" value="TTL tier-out" style="edgeLabel;html=1;fontSize=10;" vertex="1" connectable="0" parent="318">
          <mxGeometry x="0" relative="1" as="geometry"><mxPoint y="-10" as="offset"/></mxGeometry>
        </mxCell>

        <mxCell id="400" value="B4 &#183; KEY TRADE-OFFS (each choice vs its named alternative)" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;spacingLeft=4;fontSize=14;fontStyle=1;fontColor=#38761D;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="2320" width="900" height="30" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="401" value="&lt;div style=&#39;text-align:left&#39;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Push (agent) over pull (Prometheus scrape)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;Pull needs service discovery of 10 K targets and cannot capture logs at all. Push gives one uniform path for all three pillars and survives short-lived pods that die between scrapes.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Giving up:&lt;/b&gt; no free liveness signal from a failed scrape &#8212; must add explicit agent heartbeats.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Kafka over writing straight to the store&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;Decouples 550 MB/s of ingest from store availability; replay lets you rebuild a corrupted index; multiple independent consumers (indexer, alerting, sampling) read the same stream.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Giving up:&lt;/b&gt; a whole tier to operate, and ~seconds of added latency.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Separate topics per pillar over one topic&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;Different volumes (43 vs 1.7 vs 2.6 TB/day), different retention, different consumers, different partition keys. A log storm must not delay metrics &#8212; and metrics carry the alerting SLA.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Giving up:&lt;/b&gt; no cross-pillar ordering; correlation is by trace_id at query time.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=12;spacingTop=8;fillColor=#E8F1FA;strokeColor=#7EA6D9;fontSize=11;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="2355" width="760" height="330" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="402" value="&lt;div style=&#39;text-align:left&#39;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Purpose-built TSDB over ClickHouse for metrics&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;Gorilla/delta-XOR encoding gets ~2 bytes per point vs ~100 on the wire. 200 K pts/s &#215; 13 months is only feasible with that compression, and rollups are native.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Giving up:&lt;/b&gt; a second storage system to run, and no joins between metrics and logs in one query.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;ClickHouse over Elasticsearch for logs&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;At 43 TB/day, ES&#39;s inverted index costs roughly as much as the data itself and its JVM heap becomes the bottleneck. ClickHouse stores columnar + compressed (~10:1) and brute-force scans a partition fast. Most incident queries are already narrowed by service and time.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Giving up:&lt;/b&gt; fast arbitrary full-text search on rare terms &#8212; mitigated with bloom filters on trace_id and a tokenbf index on message.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Alert evaluator reads Kafka, not the TSDB&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;A 1-minute alert SLA cannot sit behind a 3-minute storage freshness SLA. Those two NFRs are in direct conflict unless alerting bypasses storage.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Giving up:&lt;/b&gt; alert evaluation sees a slightly different view than dashboards; needs care to avoid confusing on-call.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=12;spacingTop=8;fillColor=#E8F1FA;strokeColor=#7EA6D9;fontSize=11;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="840" y="2355" width="760" height="330" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="403" value="&lt;div style=&#39;text-align:left&#39;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Partition logs by hash(service, host), NOT machineId&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;machineId alone gives 10 K keys but consumers scale by partition count, and one noisy host still lands on one partition. Hashing (service, host) spreads a single service&#39;s storm across many partitions while keeping per-host ordering.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Giving up:&lt;/b&gt; no total ordering per service &#8212; acceptable, since queries sort by timestamp anyway.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Partition metrics by hash(series_id)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;Keeps all points of one series on one partition, so the metric writer can batch-compress a series without shuffling.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Partition traces by hash(trace_id)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;All spans of a trace must land together for the assembler to make a tail-sampling decision without a cross-partition join.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Tail sampling over head sampling&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;Head sampling (decide at request start) keeps a uniform 1% &#8212; which means 99% of the errors you actually needed are gone. Tail sampling buffers the trace and keeps it if any span errored or the trace was slow.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Giving up:&lt;/b&gt; assembler must buffer spans for a window; memory cost and late spans.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=12;spacingTop=8;fillColor=#E8F1FA;strokeColor=#7EA6D9;fontSize=11;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="1640" y="2355" width="760" height="330" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>

        <mxCell id="500" value="B5 &#183; DEEP DIVES &#8212; the conversations this round never reached" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;spacingLeft=4;fontSize=14;fontStyle=1;fontColor=#38761D;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="2730" width="900" height="30" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>

        <mxCell id="501" value="&lt;div style=&#39;text-align:left&#39;&gt;&lt;b&gt;THE SCALE BREAK &#8212; the 100&#215; log storm&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Setup:&lt;/b&gt; bad deploy at 14:52. 500 hosts of one service go from 100 &#8594; 10,000 lines/s. That service alone now emits 250 MB/s, on top of the 550 MB/s baseline. This is the moment engineers most need the logs.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;What breaks, in order:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;1. &lt;b&gt;Kafka partition skew.&lt;/b&gt; With the round&#39;s `machineId` key, those 500 hosts hash to at most 500 partitions but the storm is concentrated; those partitions&#39; consumers fall behind while the rest idle.&lt;br&gt;2. &lt;b&gt;Consumer lag.&lt;/b&gt; Indexer throughput is fixed. Lag grows at (arrival &#8722; drain). At +250 MB/s over a 500 MB/s drain, lag grows ~1 min per 2 min of storm.&lt;br&gt;3. &lt;b&gt;Freshness SLA violated.&lt;/b&gt; The 3-min NFR breaks first for the storming service &#8212; and it breaks silently.&lt;br&gt;4. &lt;b&gt;Blast radius.&lt;/b&gt; If all pillars share a topic or a consumer group, metrics lag too &#8594; the 1-min alert SLA dies during the exact incident it exists for.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;What the engineer at 14:54 actually sees:&lt;/b&gt; an empty or thin result set for the last few minutes &#8212; indistinguishable from &quot;the service is healthy.&quot; That is the dangerous failure: the system lies rather than admitting it is behind.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=12;spacingTop=8;fillColor=#F8CECC;strokeColor=#B85450;fontSize=11;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="2765" width="760" height="400" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>

        <mxCell id="502" value="&lt;div style=&#39;text-align:left&#39;&gt;&lt;b&gt;HOW A SENIOR HANDLES IT &#8212; five layers&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;1. Isolate by pillar.&lt;/b&gt; Separate topics and separate consumer groups so a log storm can never delay metrics. Alerting keeps working during a log flood.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;2. Quota at the gateway.&lt;/b&gt; Per-service token bucket. Over quota &#8594; 429 with `retry_after_ms`. The agent buffers to disk rather than the pipeline melting. A tenant can only hurt itself.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;3. Shed by value, not arbitrarily.&lt;/b&gt; When over quota, drop DEBUG/INFO first and always keep ERROR. Losing 90% of debug lines during an incident is fine; losing errors is not. Emit a `dropped_lines` counter so the loss is visible.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;4. Elastic consumers.&lt;/b&gt; Autoscale indexers on consumer lag, not CPU. Over-provision partitions up front (600, not 100) &#8212; you cannot increase partition count later without breaking key ordering.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;5. Never lie about freshness.&lt;/b&gt; Every query response carries `max_ts_ingested`. The UI shows &quot;data delayed 4 min for service checkout&quot; instead of an innocent empty chart. This single field is the difference between a tool on-call trusts and one they don&#39;t.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=12;spacingTop=8;fillColor=#D5E8D4;strokeColor=#82B366;fontSize=11;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="840" y="2765" width="760" height="400" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>

        <mxCell id="503" value="&lt;div style=&#39;text-align:left&#39;&gt;&lt;b&gt;OTHER DEEP DIVES A SENIOR RAISES UNASKED&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Metric cardinality explosion.&lt;/b&gt; Someone puts `user_id` or `request_id` in a tag. 2 M series becomes 200 M overnight and the TSDB index OOMs. Mitigate: per-service series budget, reject new series over the limit, alert the owning team, and expose a top-cardinality-by-service report.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Idempotency under at-least-once.&lt;/b&gt; The agent retries on timeout; the batch may already have landed. Duplicate log lines are ugly; duplicate counter increments are &lt;i&gt;wrong&lt;/i&gt;. Fix: agent-generated `X-Batch-Id` deduped at the gateway, and ship counters as monotonic absolute values, not deltas &#8212; then a replay is harmless.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Late and out-of-order events.&lt;/b&gt; An agent recovering from a 2-hour buffer replays old data into a window already rolled up. Decide: watermark + a bounded lateness window, and re-run rollups for affected windows.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Clock skew.&lt;/b&gt; Host clocks drift; a log arrives with a future timestamp. Stamp both `event_ts` (host) and `ingest_ts` (gateway); query on event_ts, alert on ingest_ts.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Monitoring the monitoring.&lt;/b&gt; The pipeline cannot be its own alerting path. A separate minimal watchdog (different region, different stack) tracks consumer lag, gateway 429 rate, agent heartbeat coverage, and end-to-end canary latency &#8212; a synthetic event injected every 10 s and timed until it is queryable.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Cost.&lt;/b&gt; 650 TB of hot logs is the dominant line item. Levers: aggressive zstd at the agent (~10:1), tiering to S3 after 3 days, sampling INFO logs at 10% while keeping 100% of ERROR, and charging teams for their own volume so the incentive is aligned.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=12;spacingTop=8;fillColor=#FFF2CC;strokeColor=#D6B656;fontSize=11;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="1640" y="2765" width="760" height="400" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>

        <mxCell id="600" value="&lt;div style=&#39;text-align:left&#39;&gt;&lt;b&gt;THE NFR CONTRADICTION NOBODY CAUGHT&lt;/b&gt;&#160;&#160;&#8212;&#160;&#160;NFR 2 says an event is visible within &lt;b&gt;3 minutes&lt;/b&gt;. NFR 3 says an alert fires within &lt;b&gt;1 minute&lt;/b&gt; of a threshold breach.&lt;br&gt;If the alert evaluator queries the same storage that dashboards read, the alert cannot beat the freshness of the data it reads &#8212; 1 min &amp;lt; 3 min is impossible. Self-raising this contradiction, and resolving it by having the evaluator consume the Kafka metrics topic directly, is exactly the &quot;owns the narrative&quot; signal a senior round is graded on.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;spacingLeft=14;spacingTop=8;fillColor=#FFF2CC;strokeColor=#D6B656;fontSize=12;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="3210" width="2360" height="110" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>

      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
</mxfile>
